area_summary:
The Electron main process is careful in places: it never opens two sockets, has a heartbeat, binds the display listeners only once, and keeps the background update checks silent. Its two biggest runtime costs are invisible, though. First, a leftover forward:true keeps a system-wide WH_MOUSE_LL hook in the main thread 24/7 on gaming PCs. Second, electron-store/conf re-reads and re-parses the whole config.json on every get() and rewrites it with fsync on every set(). That is about 12 synchronous reads plus 1 write per drop, made before the drop is forwarded, plus a few reads every 30 s while idle. On security, the app ships Electron 33, which is end-of-life, and it renders attacker-chosen media with zero clicks. Every privileged IPC (settings:set including serverUrl, which leaks the re-link token; open-external) can be called from that same overlay renderer, with no sender check and no validation. Electron's defaults for navigation, new windows and permissions are left as they are. Real bugs found:
- the WS handshake can hang forever;
- the top-guard interval leaks after rain/TTS drops and in practice never acts;
- an overlay renderer crash is never recovered;
- global Ctrl+Alt+S/M hotkeys are hijacked in production;
- autostart is broken for the portable build and overwritten by dev runs;
- a corrupt config.json causes a crash at every launch;
- the documented build-time DEFAULT_SERVER does nothing.
None of the fixes needs a wire-protocol change. Hardware acceleration is off by design, so drop rendering runs on the CPU, and renderer-side work (for example the full-screen spotlight repainted every frame) matters more than usual.

findings.proposed_change:
main.js:199 -> `overlayWin.setIgnoreMouseEvents(true);`
main.js:796-800 -> `overlayWin.setIgnoreMouseEvents(!!ignore);` (forward dropped; with no forwarding window, Electron never installs WH_MOUSE_LL).
Update the comment above the poll, and optionally overlay.html:213, which still says forward:true. Windows QA:
- hover shows the close button and the reaction bar;
- drag works;
- clicks outside drops reach the game;
- clicks go back to the game after a drag ends.

Keep electron-store only as the file backend and read from memory (call sites unchanged):
```js
const diskStore = new Store({ defaults: { /* inchangé */ }, clearInvalidConfig: true });
// conf relit/reparse config.json à chaque get() et le réécrit (fsync) à chaque set() :
// copie mémoire = seule source de lecture.
const store = (() => {
  const data = diskStore.store;                    // une seule lecture au démarrage
  const persist = () => { try { diskStore.store = data; } catch (e) { console.error('[store]', e.message); } };
  return { get: (k) => data[k], set: (k, v) => { data[k] = v; persist(); },
           setMany: (p) => { Object.assign(data, p); persist(); } };
})();
```
`diskStore.store = x` writes without the extra read that set() does.
- settings:set: one `store.setMany(patch)` instead of the per-key loop (:629).
- 'drop' case: `if (!isMuted()) showDropOnOverlay(msg); recordHistory(msg);` so the IPC goes out before the disk write.
- Optional: debounce the dropHistory persist (~2 s) and flush it in 'before-quit'.
links_update (:475-480) mutates the returned object and then calls set(), which stays correct.

findings.evidence:
main.js registers no 'will-navigate', setWindowOpenHandler or permission handler, so Electron's defaults apply:
- dropping a file or a dragged link on a window navigates it;
- the preload is injected again into whatever page it lands on;
- window.open and middle-clicks create new windows;
- every permission request (camera, mic, geolocation...) is granted.
Repro: drag an image or a browser link onto the settings window and settings.html is replaced. Closing only hides it (:251-256), so the page stays broken until the app is restarted. A remote page loaded this way gets the full window.memedrop bridge (setSettings -> serverUrl -> token sent on reconnect). The settings CSP does not apply to it.

findings.proposed_change:
```js
// ajouter `session` à l'import electron de la ligne 2
app.on('web-contents-created', (_e, contents) => {
  contents.on('will-navigate', (e) => e.preventDefault());   // nos pages sont locales et figées
  contents.setWindowOpenHandler(() => ({ action: 'deny' }));
});
// dans whenReady(), avant de créer les fenêtres :
session.defaultSession.setPermissionRequestHandler((_wc, perm, cb) => cb(perm === 'clipboard-sanitized-write'));
```
clipboard-sanitized-write is what navigator.clipboard.writeText needs for the copy-code button; neither page uses any other permission. will-navigate does not affect loadFile()/reload().

findings.evidence:
overlayWin has no 'render-process-gone' or 'did-finish-load' handler (:173-218). If its renderer dies, for example out of memory while decoding a large video with GPU disabled, the BrowserWindow survives:
- every later drop goes to a dead renderer (:352) until the app is restarted;
- if a visual drop was on screen, 'overlay:unwatch-cursor' never comes, so the 16 ms poll (:770) runs forever (62.5 wakeups/s plus IPC on each mouse move);
- the top guard never gets 'stage-empty';
- a crash during a drag leaves setIgnoreMouseEvents(false).
A manual reload (Ctrl+R in the overlay DevTools) leaves the same state out of sync.

findings.proposed_change:
```js
function resetOverlayState() {
  stopCursorPoll(); stopTopGuard();
  if (overlayWin && !overlayWin.isDestroyed()) overlayWin.setIgnoreMouseEvents(true);
}
// dans createOverlayWindow(), après loadFile :
let recentReloads = [];
overlayWin.webContents.on('did-finish-load', resetOverlayState);   // couvre aussi Ctrl+R
overlayWin.webContents.on('render-process-gone', (_e, d) => {
  console.error('[overlay] renderer gone:', d.reason);
  resetOverlayState();
  const now = Date.now();
  recentReloads = recentReloads.filter((t) => now - t < 60_000);
  if (d.reason !== 'clean-exit' && recentReloads.length < 3) { recentReloads.push(now); overlayWin.webContents.reload(); }
});
```
Also reset the capture and idle flags added by cursor-poll-while-capturing and overlay-shown-while-idle, if those are implemented.

Show the window only while something is on stage, and never give it focus:
```js
let overlayUserHidden = false, mediaOnStage = false, lastDropAt = 0, idleTimer = null;
function scheduleIdleHide() {
  clearTimeout(idleTimer);
  idleTimer = setTimeout(function check() {
    if (mediaOnStage) return;                          // 'stage-empty' relancera
    const wait = lastDropAt + 15_000 - Date.now();     // pluie <= ~4 s, toast TTS <= 12 s
    if (wait > 0) { idleTimer = setTimeout(check, wait); return; }
    if (overlayWin && !overlayWin.isDestroyed()) overlayWin.hide();
  }, 1000);
}
```
- showDropOnOverlay: `lastDropAt = Date.now(); if (payload.media) mediaOnStage = true; clearTimeout(idleTimer); if (!overlayUserHidden && !overlayWin.isVisible()) overlayWin.showInactive();`, then enforceTop()/send as today, then `if (!mediaOnStage) scheduleIdleHide();`.
- 'stage-empty': `mediaOnStage = false; scheduleIdleHide();`.
- ready-to-show: no show().
- Tray toggle: flip overlayUserHidden (hide() when set) instead of testing isVisible().

```js
function syncLoginItem(enabled) {
  if (!app.isPackaged) return;                          // dev : electron.exe sans l'app
  const exe = process.env.PORTABLE_EXECUTABLE_FILE;     // portable : le vrai .exe, pas la copie %TEMP%
